            1
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "s2",
        "kind": "explain",
        "title": "Multiplying and dividing surds",
        "body": "$$\\sqrt{a} \\times \\sqrt{b} = \\sqrt{ab} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{a}}{\\sqrt{b}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{a}{b}}$$\n\nThese work because squaring $\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{b}$ gives $\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{b}\\sqrt{b} = ab$, so $\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{b}$ must be the square root of $ab$. There is **no** rule for $\\sqrt{a} + \\sqrt{b}$: $\\sqrt{9} + \\sqrt{16} = 7$, not $\\sqrt{25}$.",
        "visuals": [
          {
            "type": "worked-example",
            "problem": "$\\sqrt{3} \\times \\sqrt{12}$",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "$= \\sqrt{3 \\times 12} = \\sqrt{36}$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$= 6$"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "check": {
          "id": "c2",
          "type": "numeric",
          "prompt": "Work out $\\sqrt{2} \\times \\sqrt{8}$.",
          "marks": 1,
          "gradeBand": "4-5",
          "skill": "multiplying surds",
          "calculator": "non-calculator",
          "tags": [],
          "solution": "$\\sqrt{16} = 4$.",
          "markScheme": [
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "marks": 1,
              "description": "4"
            }
          ],
          "discriminators": [],
          "answer": 4,
          "tolerance": 0,
          "unitsRequired": false
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "s3",
        "kind": "explain",
        "title": "Simplifying a surd",
        "body": "Look for the **largest square factor**. $48 = 16 \\times 3$, so\n\n$$\\sqrt{48} = \\sqrt{16} \\times \\sqrt{3} = 4\\sqrt{3}$$\n\nIf you pick a smaller square, $48 = 4 \\times 12$, you get $2\\sqrt{12}$ and have to simplify again. Square numbers to try: 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100.",
        "visuals": [
          {
            "type": "worked-example",
            "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{72}$",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "Square factors of 72: 4, 9, 36. Largest is 36"
              },
              {
                "text": "$\\sqrt{72} = \\sqrt{36} \\times \\sqrt{2}$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$= 6\\sqrt{2}$"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "check": {
          "id": "c3",
          "type": "short-text",
          "prompt": "Simplify $\\sqrt{50}$.",
          "marks": 1,
          "gradeBand": "6-7",
          "skill": "simplifying surds",
          "calculator": "non-calculator",
          "tags": [],
          "solution": "$50 = 25 \\times 2$, so $\\sqrt{50} = 5\\sqrt{2}$.",
          "markScheme": [
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "marks": 1,
              "description": "$5\\sqrt{2}$"
            }
          ],
          "discriminators": [],
          "accepted": [
            "5√2",
            "5 root 2",
            "5sqrt2",
            "5sqrt(2)"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "s4",
        "kind": "explain",
        "title": "Adding and subtracting surds",
        "body": "Surds add like algebra: $3\\sqrt{2} + 5\\sqrt{2} = 8\\sqrt{2}$, just as $3x + 5x = 8x$. Different surds do not combine, so simplify first to see whether they match.\n\n$$\\sqrt{8} + \\sqrt{18} = 2\\sqrt{2} + 3\\sqrt{2} = 5\\sqrt{2}$$",
        "visuals": [
          {
            "type": "worked-example",
            "problem": "$\\sqrt{75} - \\sqrt{12}$",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "$\\sqrt{75} = 5\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{12} = 2\\sqrt{3}$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$5\\sqrt{3} - 2\\sqrt{3} = 3\\sqrt{3}$"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "check": {
          "id": "c4",
          "type": "short-text",
          "prompt": "Simplify $\\sqrt{12} + \\sqrt{27}$.",
          "marks": 1,
          "gradeBand": "6-7",
          "skill": "adding surds",
          "calculator": "non-calculator",
          "tags": [],
          "solution": "$2\\sqrt{3} + 3\\sqrt{3} = 5\\sqrt{3}$.",
          "markScheme": [
            {
              "code": "B1",
              "marks": 1,
              "description": "$5\\sqrt{3}$"
            }
          ],
          "discriminators": [],
          "accepted": [
            "5√3",
            "5 root 3",
            "5sqrt3",
            "5sqrt(3)"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "s5",
        "kind": "worked-example",
        "title": "Expanding brackets with surds",
        "body": "Treat $\\sqrt{3}$ like a letter and use FOIL. The only extra fact is $\\sqrt{3} \\times \\sqrt{3} = 3$.",
        "visuals": [
          {
            "type": "worked-example",
            "problem": "Expand and simplify $(2 + \\sqrt{3})(4 - \\sqrt{3})$",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "First: $2 \\times 4 = 8$"
              },
              {
                "text": "Outer: $2 \\times (-\\sqrt{3}) = -2\\sqrt{3}$"
              },
              {
                "text": "Inner: $\\sqrt{3} \\times 4 = 4\\sqrt{3}$"
              },
              {
                "text": "Last: $\\sqrt{3} \\times (-\\sqrt{3}) = -3$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$8 - 3 - 2\\sqrt{3} + 4\\sqrt{3} = 5 + 2\\sqrt{3}$"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "s6",
        "kind": "explain",
        "title": "Rationalising the denominator",
        "body": "A surd on the bottom of a fraction counts as untidy, so exam questions ask you to clear it. Multiply top and bottom by that surd; the bottom becomes a whole number.\n\n$$\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\times \\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}$$\n\nThe value has not changed, because you multiplied by 1.",
        "visuals": [
          {
            "type": "worked-example",
            "problem": "Rationalise $\\dfrac{6}{\\sqrt{3}}$",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "$\\dfrac{6}{\\sqrt{3}} \\times \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\dfrac{6\\sqrt{3}}{3}$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$= 2\\sqrt{3}$"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "check": {
