      {
        "id": "s8",
        "kind": "grade-9",
        "title": "Grade 9: the conjugate",
        "body": "When the denominator is $3 + \\sqrt{2}$, multiplying by $\\sqrt{2}$ does not clear it. Multiply by the **conjugate** $3 - \\sqrt{2}$ instead: the surd terms cancel because $(a+b)(a-b) = a^2 - b^2$.\n\n$$\\dfrac{1}{3+\\sqrt{2}} \\times \\dfrac{3-\\sqrt{2}}{3-\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{3-\\sqrt{2}}{9-2} = \\dfrac{3-\\sqrt{2}}{7}$$\n\nExaminers also hide surds inside Pythagoras, areas, and trigonometry with exact values. Keep everything exact until the last line.",
        "visuals": [
          {
            "type": "worked-example",
            "problem": "Rationalise $\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt{5}-1}$",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "Conjugate of $\\sqrt{5}-1$ is $\\sqrt{5}+1$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$\\dfrac{4(\\sqrt{5}+1)}{(\\sqrt{5}-1)(\\sqrt{5}+1)} = \\dfrac{4(\\sqrt{5}+1)}{5-1}$"
              },
              {
                "text": "$= \\sqrt{5}+1$"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "check": {
